rent board is the standard starting position. All four available moves—(C, 4), (D, 3), (E, 6), and (F, 5)—are theoretically sound, but they lead to different opening variations. Moves (D, 3) and (E, 6) are "parallel" openings (creating a line parallel to the split between rows/cols), while (C, 4) and (F, 5) are "perpendicular" openings. Perpendicular openings are widely considered by expert players and engines to be slightly stronger or more robust for the first player, as they tend to keep the pieces tighter in the center and offer fewer immediate advantages to the opponent compared to parallel openings.\n\nReason: Among the available moves, (F, 5) is a standard perpendicular opening move. It creates a stable group of pieces in the center (flipping E5 to align with D5 and F5 horizontally) and is strategically preferred over parallel moves like (D, 3) or (E, 6) because it allows for better long-term control of the board\'s interior. Since the board is symmetric, (F, 5) is effectively equivalent to (C, 4), but it is the most common convention for the best opening play.\n\nAnswer: (F, 5)'}
